//@ compile-fail
//@ error-pattern: more than one type-argument list in a single compilation unit
// The shape that used to produce WRONG CODE rather than a rejected program.
//
// Two instantiations of one template with distinct struct type arguments. No
// nesting is involved: the arguments are plain single-segment names, so they
// mangled distinctly even before the instantiation-naming fix. What broke was
// the fallback — the second instantiation poisons the template back to its
// unspecialized body, and IR lowering has no notion of generics, so a
// struct-typed argument arrives as zero.
//
// Measured on the committed compiler (bin/madaros-linux-x86_64, Madaros
// v0.80.0), same worktree, binary swapped via SOUNIO_MADAROS_BIN:
//   before: rc=0, printed "0" and "0"
//   after:  rc=1, refused
//
// The sibling fixture specializer_multi_instantiation_nonscalar.sio covers the
// nested shape, which BOTH compilers reject — it discriminates on the message,
// not on the verdict. This one discriminates on the verdict, which is why it
// exists.
//
// See docs/handoff/BLK-20260903-specializer-nested-targ-collision.md.

struct P {
    a: i64,
}

struct Q {
    b: i64,
}

fn pick<T>(x: T, y: T) -> T {
    x
}

fn main() -> i32 with IO {
    let p: P = pick::<P>(P { a: 7 }, P { a: 9 })
    println(p.a)

    let q: Q = pick::<Q>(Q { b: 3 }, Q { b: 4 })
    println(q.b)
    0
}
